WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.738
Welcome back to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.688 --> 00:00:11.808
This is Chapter One, Episode Two: colonies, the Enlightenment, the first free constitution, and the wars over Schleswig.

3
00:00:12.358 --> 00:00:16.920
Last time, we left the Danish king with almost unlimited power.

4
00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:19.258
Now watch what Denmark does with it.

5
00:00:19.258 --> 00:00:29.858
In the 1600s and 1700s, Denmark, like many other Western European countries, became a colonial power, from about 1620.

6
00:00:29.858 --> 00:00:35.610
That means Denmark acquired land outside Denmark and declared it part of the Danish kingdom.

7
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:37.998
How did this become possible?

8
00:00:37.948 --> 00:00:56.573
Better ships, more knowledge about navigation, and successful Portuguese and Spanish voyages of discovery in the 1400s and 1500s made it possible for Europeans to cross the great oceans and sail to Asia, America and Africa, to trade and to gain power over the people there.

9
00:00:56.573 --> 00:01:04.560
A race for colonies began between Western Europe's big countries, and Great Britain gradually became the dominant colonial power.

10
00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:06.684
Denmark squeezed in where it could.

11
00:01:07.234 --> 00:01:12.097
The Danish colonial and trading activity had its eyes especially on Asia.

12
00:01:12.046 --> 00:01:18.746
Denmark set up trading colonies at Tranquebar, in Danish Trankebar, and Serampore, in present-day India.

13
00:01:18.746 --> 00:01:27.184
From there, Danish ships could carry spices, saltpetre for making gunpowder, cotton goods and other valuable goods to Europe.

14
00:01:27.184 --> 00:01:30.784
Denmark did not establish actual colonies in China.

15
00:01:30.784 --> 00:01:40.926
But trade with China, from where tea, porcelain and silk were fetched, became the largest and most profitable for the Danish merchant fleet in the 1700s.

16
00:01:41.476 --> 00:01:43.514
Now for the darker chapter.

17
00:01:43.464 --> 00:01:49.451
Danish forts were also built on the Gold Coast, on the west coast of Africa, in present-day Ghana.

18
00:01:49.451 --> 00:02:02.439
From there, in total more than one hundred thousand slaves were shipped to the Danish colonies in the West Indies, the Caribbean: Saint Thomas, Saint Croix and Saint John, on the other side of the Atlantic.

19
00:02:02.439 --> 00:02:14.551
On the Danish West Indian Islands, the enslaved people, often under physical punishment, were forced to work on the sugar cane plantations, or in the European plantation owners' houses.

20
00:02:14.551 --> 00:02:20.880
The sugar was transported to Copenhagen, where it was sold on, either in Denmark or abroad.

21
00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:24.642
The slave trade was banned from 1803.

22
00:02:24.592 --> 00:02:29.167
But until 1848, it was still allowed to keep slaves on the islands.

23
00:02:29.167 --> 00:02:36.342
Remember both years: 1803 for the ban on the trade, and 1848 for the end of keeping slaves.

24
00:02:36.342 --> 00:02:42.114
The Danish slave trade took place in the 1700s, until the ban of 1803.

25
00:02:42.664 --> 00:02:46.514
And Danish colonial history had a northern chapter, too.

26
00:02:46.464 --> 00:02:52.626
From 1721, Christian missionaries arrived in Greenland with the support of the Danish king.

27
00:02:52.626 --> 00:02:55.801
The first was the Norwegian priest Hans Egede.

28
00:02:55.801 --> 00:03:05.148
Besides the aim of making the Greenlandic population Christian, the aim was also to sell goods from Greenland, such as sealskin, blubber and whale meat.

29
00:03:05.698 --> 00:03:07.198
So how did it end?

30
00:03:07.148 --> 00:03:12.948
Denmark sold its colonies in India and Ghana to Great Britain in the middle of the 1800s.

31
00:03:12.948 --> 00:03:21.323
The Danish West Indian Islands were sold to the USA in 1917, and today they go by the name the American Virgin Islands.

32
00:03:21.323 --> 00:03:30.534
Greenland now has self-government, but is still in a so-called Realm Community, the rigsfællesskab, with Denmark, together with the Faroe Islands.

33
00:03:31.084 --> 00:03:40.084
Now we move to the 1700s, to the Age of Enlightenment, and the road to democracy, roughly seventeen hundred to eighteen forty-nine.

34
00:03:40.034 --> 00:03:44.484
The Enlightenment in eighteenth-century Europe was a time of great change.

35
00:03:44.484 --> 00:03:52.971
It was built on the idea that, through enlightenment, tolerance and critical thinking, people could become freer and better human beings.

36
00:03:52.971 --> 00:03:55.784
Reason was to lead to freedom for the individual.

37
00:03:55.784 --> 00:03:58.196
Such thoughts also reached Denmark.

38
00:03:58.196 --> 00:04:06.048
The growing middle class in the towns, the borgerskab, demanded a share in power and began to attack absolutism.

39
00:04:06.598 --> 00:04:08.848
But first, the countryside.

40
00:04:08.798 --> 00:04:14.923
In the first half of the 1700s, there was an economic crisis in Danish agriculture.

41
00:04:14.923 --> 00:04:20.948
To secure stable labour on the estates, the king introduced the stavnsbånd in 1733.

42
00:04:20.948 --> 00:04:29.373
It meant that all peasants, who made up most of the population, had to stay on the estate where they were born, until they were forty years old.

43
00:04:29.373 --> 00:04:32.098
The landowners were thereby assured labour.

44
00:04:32.098 --> 00:04:37.735
In return, the landowners had to provide some of their peasants as soldiers for the army.

45
00:04:37.735 --> 00:04:41.910
Most peasants in Denmark at that time were tenant farmers, fæstebønder.

46
00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:45.398
That means they lived on one of the landowner's smaller farms.

47
00:04:45.398 --> 00:04:53.322
The tenant farmers had to both pay a fee to the landowner and do free work on the landowner's land, to increase production on the estates.

48
00:04:53.872 --> 00:05:00.997
Towards the end of the 1700s, the ideas of the Enlightenment gradually began to break through in Denmark.

49
00:05:00.947 --> 00:05:04.534
And here comes one of the most dramatic stories in the chapter.

50
00:05:04.534 --> 00:05:14.122
King Christian the Seventh, who reigned from seventeen sixty-six to eighteen oh eight, suffered from schizophrenia and was therefore completely unfit to rule.

51
00:05:14.122 --> 00:05:24.709
The German doctor Johann Friedrich Struensee, who lived from seventeen thirty-seven to seventy-two, won the king's trust and was employed as his personal physician.

52
00:05:24.709 --> 00:05:32.268
In the years 1770 to 72 he gained great power, because the king let him govern in his place.

53
00:05:32.818 --> 00:05:36.393
Struensee issued a great number of laws on the king's behalf.

54
00:05:36.343 --> 00:05:45.280
Among other things, he introduced a high degree of freedom of the press, and abolished the use of torture to make suspects confess to a crime.

55
00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:49.330
Finally, he limited the peasants' duty to work for the local lord.

56
00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:55.734
In many ways Struensee was ahead of his time in his attempt to modernise Denmark based on Enlightenment thinking.

57
00:05:56.284 --> 00:06:07.509
But he ended up being arrested and executed, because the old holders of power, ministers, officials, nobility and others in the royal family, did not like his reforms.

58
00:06:07.459 --> 00:06:17.909
He had also had a love affair with Christian the Seventh's queen, Caroline Mathilde, who lived from seventeen fifty-one to seventy-five, and together they had a child.

59
00:06:17.909 --> 00:06:24.409
Struensee's rule therefore lasted only a short time, and most of his reforms were reversed again.

60
00:06:24.409 --> 00:06:33.720
Even so, many of his reforms, and the ideas of the Enlightenment, would regain importance for Danish society from the 1780s onwards.

61
00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:40.107
A series of reforms of agriculture and of the conditions of the rural population were carried out.

62
00:06:40.057 --> 00:06:47.945
These land reforms, landboreformerne, played a big role in the development of both Danish agriculture and Danish society.

63
00:06:47.945 --> 00:06:58.290
They meant that during the 1800s, agriculture developed from a medieval system with tenant farmers into a more modern agriculture with farmers who owned their own land.

64
00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:02.652
From 1788, the stavnsbånd was abolished.

65
00:07:02.602 --> 00:07:09.127
It meant that tenant farmers could choose for themselves whether to stay on the estate they belonged to, or to move away.

66
00:07:09.127 --> 00:07:14.852
An important result of the reforms was also that peasants got the opportunity to own land themselves.

67
00:07:14.852 --> 00:07:20.327
In many cases, landowners sold the land to the peasants who had until then rented it.

68
00:07:20.327 --> 00:07:26.690
In many cases, the village's common land was also distributed between the individual farms.

69
00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:33.227
In that way each farmer got his fields gathered in one place, so that the land could be farmed more efficiently.

70
00:07:33.227 --> 00:07:39.540
The Freedom Monument in Copenhagen marks the abolition of the stavnsbånd in 1788.

71
00:07:40.090 --> 00:07:44.165
Previously, the peasants' farms had lain together in a village.

72
00:07:44.115 --> 00:07:49.877
With the land reforms, many farms were moved out from the village to the farmer's own land.

73
00:07:49.877 --> 00:07:57.438
This move out still shapes the Danish landscape today, with farms lying scattered in the countryside, as on Bornholm.

74
00:07:57.988 --> 00:08:04.775
The land reforms created a large group of farmers with medium-sized farms, the gårdmandsklasse.

75
00:08:04.725 --> 00:08:15.600
In Denmark there were relatively more farmers in this class than in many other European countries, where a few big landowners, the lords of the manor, owned most of the land.

76
00:08:16.150 --> 00:08:24.212
Still, even though the land reforms created a large class of farmers, life in the countryside was hard for the majority.

77
00:08:24.162 --> 00:08:28.300
There was not enough land, and the land was unequally distributed.

78
00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:32.162
So only a minority of farmers had a farm they could live from.

79
00:08:32.162 --> 00:08:39.350
And a huge growth in population through the 1800s meant that more and more people could not get their own land.

80
00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:45.912
So the rural underclass grew, as the so-called husmænd, cottagers, became more numerous.

81
00:08:45.912 --> 00:08:53.525
They typically lived in smaller houses and had only small pieces of land, or none, to farm or keep animals on.

82
00:08:53.525 --> 00:08:58.600
They therefore most often worked as farm labourers on nearby farms or estates.

83
00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:03.210
Many moved into the towns to look for work, and some emigrated to America.

84
00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:06.885
Now to a golden age, and then a catastrophe.

85
00:09:06.835 --> 00:09:14.897
In the last quarter of the 1700s, Danish shipping flourished, and Copenhagen developed into an international port.

86
00:09:14.897 --> 00:09:20.460
This period is called the Florissante Period, where "florissant" means "flourishing".

87
00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:26.185
Through most of the 1700s there was peace in Denmark, which remained neutral in the wars.

88
00:09:26.185 --> 00:09:33.272
By contrast, the fleets of, among others, Great Britain, France and the Netherlands were used for war.

89
00:09:33.272 --> 00:09:38.397
At the same time, these great powers blocked each other's ports and sea routes.

90
00:09:38.397 --> 00:09:44.472
So they sent large quantities of their goods aboard neutral Danish ships, which could get through to most places.

91
00:09:44.472 --> 00:09:50.556
The ships sailed valuable cargo to Copenhagen, where it was resold to other European countries.

92
00:09:51.106 --> 00:09:55.156
This neutrality trade brought great income to the country at first.

93
00:09:55.106 --> 00:10:03.168
But in the somewhat longer term, the same trade also became the way into the Napoleonic Wars, with catastrophic consequences.

94
00:10:03.168 --> 00:10:12.593
In Denmark these wars are also called the England Wars, because Denmark became allied with France in a war against England, that is, Great Britain.

95
00:10:12.593 --> 00:10:19.302
The wars began in 1792, as a result of the French Revolution in 1789.

96
00:10:19.852 --> 00:10:29.289
In the early period of the wars, Denmark was neutral and earned well with its large merchant fleet by sailing goods to both Great Britain and France.

97
00:10:29.239 --> 00:10:38.664
The merchant fleet was protected by Denmark's own war fleet, and Great Britain was unhappy about that, because it did not want Denmark to trade with France.

98
00:10:38.664 --> 00:10:43.139
So in 1801 the British fleet attacked and defeated the Danish fleet.

99
00:10:43.139 --> 00:10:53.377
The battle is called the Battle of the Roads, Slaget på Reden, because it took place in the waters just outside Copenhagen's harbour, in the area called Copenhagen Roads.

100
00:10:53.377 --> 00:10:56.736
It took place on the second of April, 1801.

101
00:10:57.286 --> 00:11:07.749
In 1807, the French emperor Napoleon the First wanted to wage economic war on Great Britain by blocking the trade routes to and from Great Britain with warships.

102
00:11:07.699 --> 00:11:16.911
To prevent Napoleon from using the Danish ships in the blockade, the British chose to seize the whole Danish fleet, and bombarded Copenhagen in the process.

103
00:11:16.911 --> 00:11:20.173
The event is called the Bombardment of Copenhagen.

104
00:11:20.173 --> 00:11:25.122
After the defeat, Denmark was forced to choose sides, and chose France.

105
00:11:25.672 --> 00:11:32.710
The Napoleonic Wars ended with Denmark's ally, France, losing to the other European great powers.

106
00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:38.585
This meant that in a peace agreement in 1814, Denmark had to give Norway to Sweden.

107
00:11:38.585 --> 00:11:45.348
Over four hundred years of Danish-Norwegian union, since 1380, was thereby brought to an end.

108
00:11:45.898 --> 00:11:47.923
Which brings us to democracy.

109
00:11:47.873 --> 00:11:58.411
In the 1800s, Denmark was influenced by the popular and democratic currents that, among other things, came out of the French Revolution of 1789.

110
00:11:58.411 --> 00:12:02.761
In 1848 revolution broke out in many European capitals.

111
00:12:02.761 --> 00:12:08.223
There was no revolution in Denmark, but the unrest around Europe affected the country.

112
00:12:08.223 --> 00:12:20.161
In March 1848, the middle class in Copenhagen demanded that King Frederik the Seventh, who reigned from eighteen forty-eight to sixty-three, should introduce a free constitution.

113
00:12:20.161 --> 00:12:25.470
Under pressure from the agitated public mood, the king chose to follow the demand.

114
00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:31.745
He abolished the absolute form of government and declared himself a constitutional king.

115
00:12:31.695 --> 00:12:37.808
That means he would govern in cooperation with the people, on the basis of the constitution, the Grundlov.

116
00:12:37.808 --> 00:12:46.683
On the fifth of June, 1849, Frederik the Seventh signed Denmark's first free constitution, "Danmarks Riges Grundlov".

117
00:12:46.683 --> 00:12:51.864
Remember that key date for the exam: the fifth of June, 1849.

118
00:12:52.414 --> 00:13:01.852
With the constitution, Denmark got a parliament with two legislative assemblies, the Folketing and the Landsting, which together were called the Rigsdag.

119
00:13:01.802 --> 00:13:11.754
After almost two hundred years, since sixteen sixty to sixty-one, under absolute kings, part of the Danish population now had direct influence on legislation.

120
00:13:12.304 --> 00:13:13.467
Who could vote?

121
00:13:13.417 --> 00:13:20.854
Men over thirty with their own household, that is, who either owned or rented their own home, could elect members of the Rigsdag.

122
00:13:20.854 --> 00:13:28.829
To be elected to the Folketing, you had to be twenty-five years old, while members of the Landsting had to be forty and have a high income.

123
00:13:28.829 --> 00:13:34.504
Neither women nor the poor had the right to vote, or could be elected to either of the two chambers.

124
00:13:34.504 --> 00:13:41.304
All in all, the constitution meant that about fifteen percent of the population over thirty had the right to vote.

125
00:13:41.304 --> 00:13:49.044
Nevertheless, the constitution was very democratic and liberal compared with most other states' constitutions at the time.

126
00:13:49.594 --> 00:13:52.194
Now it is time for your exam practice.

127
00:13:52.144 --> 00:13:57.057
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

128
00:13:57.057 --> 00:14:04.542
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

129
00:14:05.092 --> 00:14:06.342
Question 1.

130
00:14:06.292 --> 00:14:12.980
In which century did the Danish slave trade between Africa and the Danish colonies in the Caribbean take place?

131
00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:15.580
Option A: The 1100s.

132
00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:18.242
Option B: The 1400s.

133
00:14:18.242 --> 00:14:21.144
Option C: The 1700s.
[DA] I hvilket af følgende århundreder fandt dansk slavehandel mellem Afrika og de danske kolonier i Caribien sted?  A: 1100-tallet  |  B: 1400-tallet  |  C: 1700-tallet

134
00:14:27.694 --> 00:14:30.632
The answer is C: The 1700s.

135
00:14:30.582 --> 00:14:35.082
It appeared in the May 2023 and November 2024 exams.
[DA] ✔ C: 1700-tallet

136
00:14:35.632 --> 00:14:36.882
Question 2.

137
00:14:36.832 --> 00:14:40.920
Which country was the Danish West Indies sold to in 1917?

138
00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:42.770
Option A: France.

139
00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:45.057
Option B: Great Britain.

140
00:14:45.057 --> 00:14:47.388
Option C: The USA.
[DA] Hvilket land blev Dansk Vestindien solgt til i 1917?  A: Frankrig  |  B: Storbritannien  |  C: USA

141
00:14:53.938 --> 00:14:56.276
The answer is C: The USA.

142
00:14:56.226 --> 00:15:01.014
It appeared in the November 2022 and November 2025 exams.
[DA] ✔ C: USA

143
00:15:01.564 --> 00:15:02.902
Question 3.

144
00:15:02.852 --> 00:15:08.839
Which population group had its rights limited by the introduction of the stavnsbånd in 1733?

145
00:15:08.839 --> 00:15:11.377
Option A: The middle class.

146
00:15:11.377 --> 00:15:13.564
Option B: The peasants.

147
00:15:13.564 --> 00:15:15.840
Option C: The nobility.
[DA] Hvilken befolkningsgruppe fik begrænset deres rettigheder med indførelsen af stavnsbåndet i 1733?  A: Borgerskabet  |  B: Bønderne  |  C: Adelen

148
00:15:22.390 --> 00:15:24.753
The answer is B: The peasants.

149
00:15:24.703 --> 00:15:29.106
It appeared in the June 2021 and November 2022 exams.
[DA] ✔ B: Bønderne

150
00:15:29.656 --> 00:15:30.956
Question 4.

151
00:15:30.906 --> 00:15:34.069
What did the stavnsbånd of the 1700s involve?

152
00:15:34.069 --> 00:15:39.181
Option A: That the king had the exclusive right to trade with foreign countries.

153
00:15:39.181 --> 00:15:42.806
Option B: That peasants had a limited right to move.

154
00:15:42.806 --> 00:15:47.268
Option C: That Christianity was the only permitted religion.
[DA] Hvad indebar stavnsbåndet i 1700-tallet?  A: At kongen havde eneret på at handle med udlandet  |  B: At bønderne havde begrænset ret til at flytte  |  C: At kristendommen var den eneste tilladte religion

155
00:15:53.818 --> 00:15:57.781
The answer is B: That peasants had a limited right to move.

156
00:15:57.731 --> 00:16:00.702
It appeared in the November 2025 exam.
[DA] ✔ B: At bønderne havde begrænset ret til at flytte

157
00:16:01.252 --> 00:16:02.715
Question 5.

158
00:16:02.665 --> 00:16:06.452
Which country was Denmark in union with until 1814?

159
00:16:06.452 --> 00:16:08.290
Option A: Sweden.

160
00:16:08.290 --> 00:16:10.302
Option B: Estonia.

161
00:16:10.302 --> 00:16:12.288
Option C: Norway.
[DA] Hvilket land var Danmark i union med indtil 1814?  A: Sverige  |  B: Estland  |  C: Norge

162
00:16:18.838 --> 00:16:21.001
The answer is C: Norway.

163
00:16:20.951 --> 00:16:25.242
It appeared in the June 2021 and May 2023 exams.
[DA] ✔ C: Norge

164
00:16:25.792 --> 00:16:27.230
Question 6.

165
00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:33.405
Which country defeated Denmark in the Battle of the Roads in 1801, during the Napoleonic Wars?

166
00:16:33.405 --> 00:16:35.755
Option A: Great Britain.

167
00:16:35.755 --> 00:16:37.955
Option B: The Netherlands.

168
00:16:37.955 --> 00:16:39.924
Option C: Spain.
[DA] Hvilket land blev Danmark angrebet og besejret af i Slaget på Reden i 1801 under Napoleonskrigene?  A: Storbritannien  |  B: Nederlandene  |  C: Spanien

169
00:16:46.474 --> 00:16:49.112
The answer is A: Great Britain.

170
00:16:49.062 --> 00:16:51.942
It appeared in the November 2024 exam.
[DA] ✔ A: Storbritannien

171
00:16:52.492 --> 00:16:53.930
Question 7.

172
00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:56.480
What happened in Denmark in 1807?

173
00:16:56.480 --> 00:16:59.617
Option A: Denmark had to cede Schleswig.

174
00:16:59.617 --> 00:17:03.417
Option B: The king signed Denmark's Constitution.

175
00:17:03.417 --> 00:17:06.960
Option C: The British bombarded Copenhagen.
[DA] Hvilken begivenhed fandt sted i Danmark i 1807?  A: Danmark måtte afstå Slesvig  |  B: Kongen underskrev Danmarks Riges Grundlov  |  C: Briterne bombarderede København

176
00:17:13.510 --> 00:17:17.248
The answer is C: The British bombarded Copenhagen.

177
00:17:17.198 --> 00:17:20.106
It appeared in the November 2021 exam.
[DA] ✔ C: Briterne bombarderede København

178
00:17:20.656 --> 00:17:21.919
Question 8.

179
00:17:21.869 --> 00:17:24.856
When did Denmark's Constitution come into force?

180
00:17:24.856 --> 00:17:27.494
Option A: 1849.

181
00:17:27.494 --> 00:17:30.106
Option B: 1864.

182
00:17:30.106 --> 00:17:32.748
Option C: 1899.
[DA] Hvornår trådte Danmarks Riges Grundlov i kraft?  A: 1849  |  B: 1864  |  C: 1899

183
00:17:39.298 --> 00:17:42.136
The answer is A: 1849.

184
00:17:42.086 --> 00:17:47.118
It appeared in 4 different exams, most recently in June 2026.
[DA] ✔ A: 1849

185
00:17:47.668 --> 00:17:49.043
Question 9.

186
00:17:48.993 --> 00:17:51.093
What happened in 1849?

187
00:17:51.093 --> 00:17:53.768
Option A: Denmark lost Skåne.

188
00:17:53.768 --> 00:17:56.943
Option B: The stavnsbånd was abolished.

189
00:17:56.943 --> 00:18:00.192
Option C: The constitution was introduced.
[DA] Hvad skete i 1849?  A: Danmark mistede Skåne  |  B: Stavnsbåndet blev ophævet  |  C: Grundloven blev indført

190
00:18:06.742 --> 00:18:10.155
The answer is C: The constitution was introduced.

191
00:18:10.105 --> 00:18:12.978
